SUMMARY:Econometrics – Rami V. Tabri (Monash) – Ordinal Distributional 
 Change and Conservative Transition Benchmarks: Measurement\, Identificatio
 n\, and Inference
LOCATION:Room 304\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3053
DESCRIPTION:Rami V. Tabri: “Ordinal Distributional Change and Conservativ
 e Transition Benchmarks: Measurement\, Identification\, and Inference”\n
 https://sites.google.com/site/ramivtabri/\n\nZoom: https://unimelb.zoom.us
 /j/85966225829?pwd=g6hOdHyvYB0qta0aFzGbWOgczJ1Qzr.1\n\nGoogle Maps: https:
 //maps.app.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nRepeated cross-sections 
 reveal changes in ordinal distributions but not the transitions producing 
 them. I axiomatically characterize a threshold-weighted probability metric
  for ordinal change from threshold-crossing principles. For any threshold 
 additive ordinal geometry\, the discrepancy coincides with the Wasserstein
 –1 distance induced by that ground metric and measures minimum displacem
 ent\; its optimizing plans define conservative transition benchmarks. With
  missing outcomes\, I derive sharp identified sets for the discrepancy and
  endpoint-conditioned benchmark plans. I develop finite-sample-valid proje
 ction inference using randomized Monte Carlo calibration and global search
  with an almost-sure convergence guarantee. Applied to Arab Barometer data
 \, the framework documents a robust shift toward broader and more regular 
 remittance receipt in Lebanon. The discrepancy interval remains well\nsepa
 rated from zero after allowing for item nonresponse and sampling uncertain
 ty\, while benchmark bounds provide strong numerical evidence that least-d
 isplacement restructuring excludes movement toward less frequent receipt a
 nd requires reassignment from nonreceipt to recurrent receipt.
URL:https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/85966225829?pwd=g6hOdHyvYB0qta0aFzGbWOgczJ1Qz
 r.1
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2f4eaee1b8038e85803a7bf0f446762e@generated
DTSTAMP:20261020T214746Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261020T214746Z
SEQUENCE:0
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Melbourne:20261023T100000
DTEND;TZID=Australia/Melbourne:20261023T120000
SUMMARY:Econometrics – Mitchell O’Hara-Wild (Monash) – Graphs for for
 ecasting many related time series
LOCATION:Room 404\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3053
DESCRIPTION:Mitchell O’Hara-Wild: “Graphs for forecasting many related 
 time series”\nhttps://mitchelloharawild.com/\n\nZoom: https://unimelb.zo
 om.us/j/85966225829?pwd=g6hOdHyvYB0qta0aFzGbWOgczJ1Qzr.1\n\nGoogle Maps: h
 ttps://maps.app.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nForecasting in prac
 tice rarely involves a single time series. Organisations forecast large co
 llections of related series\, such as product sales across stores and regi
 ons\, which requires software that handles many series and models consiste
 ntly and produces probabilistic forecasts that can be evaluated\, combined
 \, and compared. The fable R package addresses this by treating forecastin
 g as a workflow of composable stages designed with tidy data analysis prin
 ciples. The relationships between related time series are commonly encoded
  with aggregation constraint matrices\, which forecast reconciliation uses
  to produce coherent and more accurate forecasts. Directed acyclic graphs 
 (DAGs) offer a more general representation that make large collections of 
 series easier to explore and manipulate. As series are disaggregated by ma
 ny attributes\, their number grows rapidly\, increasing computational cost
  and degrading accuracy through noisy\, uninformative\, or misspecified se
 ries. Using time series features and graph search algorithms\, uninformati
 ve subgraphs can be efficiently pruned while preserving coherency\, substa
 ntially reducing the number of series to forecast while retaining most of 
 the information. This seminar begins with the software design of fable for
  forecasting at scale\, then introduces graph-based forecast reconciliatio
 n and feature-based graph pruning for accurate forecasting at scale.
URL:https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/85966225829?pwd=g6hOdHyvYB0qta0aFzGbWOgczJ1Qz
 r.1
END:VEVENT
